  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Markets for credible national sustainability tools</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Building understanding and uptake of credible national legality instruments and sustainability mechanisms through aligning producer- and consumer-country credibility expectations and practice. Working in Indonesia, Ghana, China, and India.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Direct beneficiaries are national system actors, policymakers, and businesses in producer and consumer countries. Indirectly, smallholders and local communities benefit through stronger, more inclusive governance and better market access. The project focuses on legality, credibility, transparency, and accountability, supporting policy reform and institutional processes to promote responsible sourcing and reduce illegal deforestation.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will strengthen the credibility, traceability, and interoperability of producer-country legality and sustainability mechanisms to increase their recognition by importing countries, and to improve their policy and market integration in China and India. It will enhance systems linked to traceability, legality, and forest governance for timber, cocoa, and palm in Indonesia and Ghana, while building demand-side understanding and uptake of these mechanisms in China and India. The project will also generate evidence on effective ways to strengthen local forest governance. Activities include stakeholder mapping, research, technical guidance, capacity building, policy dialogues, interviews, and learning through evidence generation.</narrative>
  </description>
